<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurukh people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oraon people, Oraon, Oraons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oraon, who call themselves Kurukh, are an Adivasi people of eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. They speak Kurukh, a Dravidian language, and traditionally farmed the uplands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur</a> plateau. Many adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> through Lutheran, Catholic and Anglican missions from the 19th century, and they are one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurukh-people.jpg" alt="Kurukh people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rocky international · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukh_dance_of_Oraons_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Edward Tuite Dalton, Oraon is a name given by their Munda neighbours, meaning to roam, while Kurukh may derive from a word meaning to shout, so that it would mean a speaker. Their origins are debated. The Indian Anthropological Society places their first home in the Konkan, and they are said to have settled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur Plateau</a> by 100 CE. Sarat Chandra Roy suggested a migration from Coorg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, another hypothesis links them with the Brahui of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balochistan">Baluchistan</a>, and a third places them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>.</p><p>Oraon traditions recorded by Dalton tell of expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, defeat at Kalinjar, and a stay at Rohtasgarh before they were driven out by Muslims in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and settled in Chotanagpur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under British rule the Kurukh rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> over taxation: Budhu Bhagat led the Lakra rebellion, also known as the Kol uprising, in 1832. Colonial policies left most of the tribe working as agricultural labourers for zamindars by the early 20th century, and many migrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> gardens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, where they were called Hill Coolies. From 1914 to 1920 Jatra Bhagat led the Tana Bhagat movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>, whose followers later supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Kurukh are patrilineal and patrilocal, divided into exogamous clans named after plants, animals and objects, such as Lakra (tiger), Tirkey (an eagle) and Toppo (woodpecker), which also serve as surnames. A village is governed through offices such as the Pahan, the village priest; young people were taught in the youth dormitory, the Dhumkuria, and disputes are settled at the Akhra, the meeting place. Twelve to thirty villages form a Parha council, presided over by the headman of the Raja village.</p><p>Just under half still speak Kurukh as their mother tongue, many having adopted Sadri or Odia. Unlike many neighbouring peoples who practise shifting cultivation, they plough their fields. Of the 3,623,512 counted in the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, about 36 per cent were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, 30 per cent Christian and 29 per cent followers of Sarna, the nature religion of the plateau, whose rituals are held in sacred groves and whose supreme god is Dharmesh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kurukh people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gangpur was a large, forested state with a mainly Adivasi population, including <b>Oraon</b>, Munda and Kharia peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/gangpur-state">Gangpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Raja ruled a population largely of <b>Oraon</b>, Kanwar and other Adivasi communities, with an economy of upland rice, forest produce and tea.</blockquote><a href="/state/jashpur-state">Jashpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maharajas governed a vast upland of sal forest, with <b>Oraon</b>, Gond, Kanwar and other Adivasi communities forming much of the population.</blockquote><a href="/state/surguja-state">Surguja State</a></li><li><blockquote>The state was hilly, forested and largely Adivasi, with <b>Oraon</b>, Kanwar and Gond communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/udaipur-state-chhattisgarh">Udaipur State (Chhattisgarh)</a></li></ul></section>
